ght;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">48%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">May</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">68%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jun</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">84%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Jul</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">90%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Aug</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">82%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Sep</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">58%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Oct</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">42%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Nov</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">24%</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">Dec</td><td style="padding:.3rem .5rem;text-align:right;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.05)">16%</td></tr></tbody></table><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.74rem;color:#6d675d;line-height:1.5;margin-top:.5rem">Illustrative seasonal pattern for Grand Strand short-term rentals. Garden City's oceanfront and pier-adjacent houses skew heavily to the summer peak.</p></figure><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:8px;padding:1.5rem 1.6rem;margin:2rem 0"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.62rem;letter-spacing:.18em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:1rem">Short-term rental data &middot; Garden City Beach</p><div style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit,minmax(112px,1fr));gap:1.2rem 1rem;margin-bottom:1.15rem"><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$33,942</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average annual revenue</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">36.2%</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">occupancy</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">$372</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">average nightly rate</div></div><div><div style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.55rem;color:var(--navy);line-height:1.1">395</div><div style="font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.05em;color:var(--muted);margin-top:.15rem">active listings</div></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.87rem;line-height:1.65;margin:0">The strongest tenth of listings here gross $9,411 a month or more; the median listing makes $2,916. June is the strongest month and January the weakest. Averages cover every listing in the market, including part-time ones, so a well-run property can sit well above them. <a href="/invest/airbnb-income/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Compare all nine submarkets</a>. <span style="font-size:.78rem">AirROI, data through 2026-08-08.</span></p></div><h2>How rental returns work in Garden City</h2><p>The return profile is heavily seasonal, so the numbers live or die on the annual occupancy assumption. Peak-summer weeks on a group-friendly oceanfront house produce most of the year's revenue, while winter carries cost with little income. Build the model on a conservative full-year occupancy, subtract the real expense stack of a large vacation home, and judge the deal on net operating income. Inlet-side homes trade some peak ceiling for a steadier, lower-cost profile.</p><h2>Financing an investment property in Garden City</h2><p>Large vacation houses and condos finance differently. Houses usually qualify more conventionally; condos hinge on project warrantability and reserves. Investor and second-home loans require larger down payments, and DSCR financing on projected rents is common for the bigger short-term-rental houses. Given the flood exposure on the peninsula, lenders will also want the insurance settled early.</p><h2>Garden City rules and costs that move the numbers</h2><p>Three line items move a Garden City deal. South Carolina's 6 percent assessment ratio on non-owner-occupied property raises the tax bill above what a primary residence would pay, and Chapter3 will confirm the parcel's current figure. Short-term rentals carry state and local accommodations taxes and county licensing. Because the peninsula is exposed to water on both sides, flood and wind insurance are recurring costs. Put them in the model from the start rather than discovering them at closing.</p><h2>The Garden City investor thesis</h2><ol class="thesis-list"><li><span class="thesis-num">01</span><span class="thesis-text">Group-friendly oceanfront beach houses near the pier are the highest-revenue play in peak season.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">02</span><span class="thesis-text">Inlet and creek-front homes with dock access are a defensible niche at a lower entry than oceanfront.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">03</span><span class="thesis-text">A largely unincorporated setting has historically meant a more permissive vacation-rental environment, subject to county rules.</span></li><li><span class="thesis-num">04</span><span class="thesis-text">Knowing which side of the peninsula a property sits on is half the analysis.</span></li></ol><h2>What a first Garden City deal looks like</h2><p>A first Garden City purchase usually comes down to which side of the peninsula fits your plan. An oceanfront or near-pier house aimed at summer group rentals carries the highest ceiling and the heaviest seasonality, so the model has to survive a conservative annual occupancy. An inlet or creek-side home with dock access trades some peak revenue for a steadier, lower-cost profile. Either way, settle the flood insurance early and confirm the county rules for the exact parcel before you commit.</p><h2>What to watch before you buy in Garden City</h2><ul style="padding-left:1.1rem;margin-top:.5rem"><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Heavy seasonality.</strong> Revenue concentrates in summer. Model the full year at a conservative occupancy, including the slow months.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>Flood and wind exposure.</strong> The peninsula is exposed on both sides. Insurance is a real line item; get quotes before you offer.</li><li style="margin-bottom:.7rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7"><strong>County versus town rules.</strong> Rules differ across the unincorporated and incorporated lines nearby. Confirm jurisdiction for the parcel.</li></ul><h2>Where Garden City sits, and why being unincorporated matters</h2><p>Garden City Beach is an unincorporated community about 10 to 12 miles south of Myrtle Beach on U.S. 17, a 20 to 25 minute drive. It is between Surfside Beach to the north and Murrells Inlet to the south. It straddles two counties, mostly Horry with a southern tip in Georgetown. Because it is unincorporated, there is no city government imposing its own short-term-rental ban, so it follows the more permissive county framework, which is exactly why it behaves differently from the City of Myrtle Beach for investors.</p><h2>The Garden City market right now</h2><p>Garden City is a genuine oceanfront and vacation-rental market. Oceanfront and ocean-view condos along North and South Waccamaw Drive commonly trade from the low $200,000s to about $400,000. Waters Edge, Royal Garden, and Sea Watch are examples of those buildings. Raised beach houses with four to seven bedrooms and oceanfront single-family homes run from the $600,000s past $1 million. For context on the segment, Horry County oceanfront and resort condos were reported around a $238,825 median in early 2026 with days on market near 162, meaning buyers have room to negotiate. <!-- TIMMY: current CCAR MLS median, DOM, and price/sqft for Garden City oceanfront condo vs raised beach home. --></p><h2>Short-term rental rules in Garden City</h2><p>Short-term rentals are generally allowed in Garden City because it is unincorporated, so there is no municipal STR ban of the kind the City of Myrtle Beach enforces. Properties follow Horry County rules plus any HOA restrictions. The southern tip follows Georgetown County rules instead. That makes Garden City one of the more STR-friendly oceanfront options on the Strand. You still collect and remit accommodations tax, and an HOA or condo regime can restrict rentals, and Chapter3 will verify the specific building or community rules before you count on nightly income. <!-- TIMMY: which Garden City buildings/HOAs restrict rentals, and which run the strongest on-site rental programs. --> Every jurisdiction's licenses and taxes: <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">short-term rental rules by city</a>.</p><h2>Owning in Garden City: the 4 percent versus 6 percent property tax rule</h2><p>South Carolina assesses an owner-occupied primary residence at a 4 percent ratio, and it does not pay the school operating tax. A second home or investment is assessed at 6 percent and pays the school operating tax. In Garden City that makes a second home's tax bill about 3.2 times a primary home's on the same house. See the <a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Revenue</a> for the assessment-ratio rules. Full county math, with a calculator: <a href="/buyers/property-taxes/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">Horry County property taxes</a>.</p><h2>Flood zones and insurance in Garden City</h2><p>Garden City is a narrow strip between the ocean and the inlet, so flood exposure is location-specific. Oceanfront and inlet-front lots sit in FEMA flood zones that require flood insurance on a federally backed loan, and wind coverage is a meaningful line item. Raised construction and newer builds generally price better on insurance. Pull the FEMA flood map for the exact address. <!-- TIMMY: which streets flood, and current flood/wind premium ranges by section. --> What the three coastal policies cost: <a href="/buyers/coastal-insurance/" style="color:var(--brass);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none">coastal home insurance costs</a>.</p><h2>Garden City lifestyle and amenities</h2><p>Garden City's landmark is the Garden City Pier, a 650-plus-foot fishing pier with a tackle shop, arcade, cafe, and the "End of the Pier" bar with live music in season. The Garden City Inlet nature preserve offers crabbing and kayaking, the Village Surf Shoppe is a local institution, and the Murrells Inlet MarshWalk restaurants are minutes south. It is a golf-cart-friendly, laid-back beach community, and Myrtle Beach International Airport is only about 10 miles away.</p><h2>Schools serving Garden City</h2><p>Public schools on the Horry County side generally fall within <a href="https://www.horrycountyschools.net/about-hcs/our-schools" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County Schools</a>, including Seaside Elementary, St. James Middle, and St. James High; the small Georgetown County portion is served by the Georgetown County School District. Assignment is by address, and Chapter3 will confirm the exact schools on the district boundary map.</p><h2>What it is like to live in Garden City</h2><p>Garden City is a narrow, golf-cart-friendly strip between the ocean and the inlet, more laid-back than a resort town. The Garden City Pier anchors the community with its tackle shop, arcade, and end-of-pier bar, the inlet nature preserve offers crabbing and kayaking, and the Murrells Inlet MarshWalk restaurants are minutes south. Myrtle Beach International Airport is only about 10 miles away.</p><p>For a live-in buyer, the tradeoff is proximity to the sand against a busy summer season and coastal flood exposure. Oceanfront and inlet-front lots sit in FEMA flood zones, and Chapter3 will check the flood map and insurance for the exact address before you commit.</p><h2>Buying in Garden City as a primary residence</h2><p>Garden City is best known as an oceanfront rental market, but it also suits second-home owners and full-time residents who want to be steps from the sand without big-city density. Because it is unincorporated, it does not have a town STR ban, so if you want a quiet street you will depend on the HOA rules rather than zoning. A primary residence is taxed at 4% with the owner-occupant credit; a second home at 6% without it.</p><h2>Read the Garden City HOA rules before you commit to a house</h2><p>In a market with this many associations, the covenants matter as much as the house. Before you make an offer, read the HOA bylaws and rules, because they can restrict short-term and long-term rentals, pets, parking, golf carts, fences, and exterior changes. The classic trap: you have spent years training your dog to walk off leash, then buy into a community with a strict leash rule and lose the thing you valued most. Read the documents before you make an offer, not after.</p><p>Ask us and we will get you the governing documents for any Garden City community, from our own files if we already have them or by requesting them for you if we do not. It is also why a Chapter3 agent asks a lot of questions about how you live. We would rather spend the time up front than match you to a home with a rule or an HOA that blocks what you enjoy or turns out to be a poor investment.</p><h2>Garden City community stats</h2><p>Official figures from government sources, with no interpretation added. Each links to its source so you can read the full data yourself.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">Population &amp; income &middot; <a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/gardencitycdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Population (2020 Census)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 9,357</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median household income (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/gardencitycdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Persons in poverty (ACS)</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/gardencitycdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></span></div><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin:1.25rem 0 .5rem">Cost, taxes &amp; risk &middot; government sources</p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Property assessment ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">4% primary / 6% second home &nbsp;<a href="https://dor.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC DOR</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Median home value</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://www.census.gov/quickfacts/fact/table/gardencitycdpsouthcarolina" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Census Bureau</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Flood hazard by address</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://msc.fema.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FEMA Flood Map Service Center</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Crime statistics</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a> &middot; <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a></span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">School performance</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right"><a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Dept. of Education report cards</a></span></div></div><p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.85rem;line-height:1.7">How to pull the crime numbers yourself: on the <a href="https://cde.ucr.cjis.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">FBI Crime Data Explorer</a>, choose South Carolina, then select the local agency to see reported offenses by year. The local agency is the county sheriff's office or the city police department. On <a href="https://www.sled.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SLED</a>, open the current "Crime in South Carolina" report and find the county and agency tables. Chapter3 will pull the current figures for a specific address with you.</p><h2>Garden City school stats</h2><p>Garden City straddles two counties. Horry-side addresses are generally served by Seaside Elementary, St. James Middle, and St. James High in Horry County Schools; the small Georgetown-side tip by the Georgetown County School District. Figures are from the South Carolina Department of Education report card and federal education data, with no interpretation added.</p><div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--rule);border-radius:12px;padding:1.25rem 1.4rem;margin:1rem 0"><p style="font-size:.72rem;letter-spacing:.1em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.5rem">St. James High School (Horry side) &middot; <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC report card</a></p><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Grades served</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">9-12</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Enrollment</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 1,880</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Four-year graduation rate</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">about 87% (state average about 84%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Math proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">89% (state average 43%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Reading and writing proficiency</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">88% (state average 52%)</span></div><div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:1rem;padding:.55rem 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(28,32,40,.06)"><span style="color:var(--navy)">Student-to-teacher ratio</span><span style="color:var(--muted);text-align:right">18 to 1</span></div></div><p>For the elementary and middle schools that feed it, and for any exact address, read the full report cards at the <a href="https://screportcards.ed.sc.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">South Carolina Department of Education</a>. The report card also publishes SAT and ACT averages, Advanced Placement and dual-enrollment participation, college-and-career readiness, dropout rate, and chronic absenteeism. Ask your Chapter3 agent and we will pull the report card for the school an address is zoned to.</p><h2>Who Garden City suits</h2><p>Garden City is built for the oceanfront short-term-rental investor and the vacation-home buyer. Because it is unincorporated and STR-friendly, the nightly-rental math that does not work inside the City of Myrtle Beach often works here. It also suits second-home owners who want to be steps from the sand without big-city density. If you specifically want an easy STR path on the ocean, this is one of the strongest fits on the Strand.</p></div>
